    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d250">

                <pb facs="0302" n="296" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg296"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">852.00/10140: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Hayes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-22T11:00:00+01:00">September 22,
                            1942—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 22—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1407. My 1406, September 22.<anchor n="56" xml:id="d250-f1"/> Government’s
                    statement of foreign policy is nearly identical with previous similar statements
                    except that the reference to historical solidarity with Hispanic-American
                    countries is new. In view of the fact that all but two of the Hispanic-American
                    countries are either at war or have broken relations with the Axis, this
                    addition to the statement of Spanish foreign policy is very encouraging to our
                    side. Likewise very significant and favorable to us is the emphasis on
                    solidarity with Portugal coming so closely after the Portuguese Government’s
                    statement of moral solidarity with Brazil upon the latter’s entry into the
                    war.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hayes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
